      { "text": "A grade 1 spondylolisthesis reducing the height of the neural foramen", "explanation": "Incorrect. A listhesis is diagnosed by tracing the posterior vertebral body line and finding forward translation, roughly 25% for a grade 1, not by foraminal appearance alone. No step-off is described. This would be the correct answer if the posterior body line showed about a quarter body width of anterior slip." },
      { "text": "Beam divergence, so the endplates and laminae are seen overlapping", "explanation": "Correct. The x-ray beam behaves like a line of sight: where it is centred you look straight at the endplates so they are crisp and the superimposed laminae leave the foramen looking wide. Levels away from centre are viewed obliquely, so you look through the endplate surfaces and they overlap, making the interspace appear falsely narrow, while staggered laminae appear to encroach on the foramen. The lecturer's kitchen table analogy captures it: squatting you see only the edge, standing you see the edge plus the surface. Reading this projection artifact as pathology in an asymptomatic segment is a common error." }
