      { "text": "Facet cartilage only begins to wear after the disc has lost a critical volume", "explanation": "Incorrect. This describes a later step in the degenerative cascade, in which lost disc height compresses the facets and accelerates cartilage wear. It explains facet arthritis, not the delayed onset of discogenic pain. This would be the correct answer if the patient presented with arthritic facet pain years after a documented herniation." },
      { "text": "Only the outer annulus is innervated, and the tear has now reached it", "explanation": "Correct. Most of the disc is insensate; sensory innervation is confined to the periphery of the annulus fibrosus. Radial tears begin centrally and propagate outward, so patients can damage a disc for years without symptoms. Once the tear and the nuclear material it carries reach that innervated outer ring, the inflammatory mediators within the nucleus irritate it and pain begins. This is why so many patients attribute the problem to a single lifting event that was in fact only the last of many." },
      { "text": "Inflammatory mediators arise from the vertebral endplate rather than the disc", "explanation": "Incorrect. The inflammatory mediators in question are carried within the nucleus pulposus itself, which is why contact with the outer annulus or with a nerve root produces pain. Endplate inflammation (Modic change) is a downstream consequence of lost disc height. This would be the correct answer if the question described bone marrow signal change on MRI after height loss." }
